1. Global stack
Use global stack (array of Node pointers). reduce() must return stack balanced.
Numerals may be put on stack too (fixed positions at the top). GC: autoprotect
everything that's on stack (so, the list of protected nodes and "immortal" flag
become unneeded).
EXPECTED PERFORMANCE INCREASE: NONE/VERY LOW

2. Custom allocator
Do not use malloc/free for Node allocation. Allocate big chunks, chain all free
nodes into single-linked list. Allocation is just picking of the first element
of the list.
EXPECTED PERFORMANCE INCREASE: LOW

3. No hash table
Hash table allows to avoid duplicates but requires additional time for other
manipulations. Remove it completely (or, probably, use only during program
parsing). All non-free nodes must be chained to allow GC.
EXPECTED PERFORMANCE INCREASE: QUESTIONABLE

4. Do not change in-place, keep pointer to reduced form
It was the first version, and it worked too slowly for some reason. Try again,
find the bug. Refusal of in-place modifications should reduce number of hashings
and rehashings.
EXPECTED PERFORMANCE INCREASE: QUESTIONABLE

5. Make numerals on demand
It may speed up GC a little bit, especially for small programs without input.
But would decrease performance of programs with input.
EXPECTED PERFORMANCE INCREASE: NONE


